---
import { contactEmail } from '@/components/site/links'

import './privacy.css'

const mailto = `mailto:${contactEmail}`

// MHMDA requires the Washington complaint route in an appeal denial.
const washingtonComplaint = 'https://www.atg.wa.gov/file-complaint'
---

<!-- Washington's MHMDA wants this policy on its own page, with only the items that the Act requires. See docs/privacy-and-intake.md. -->
<article class="privacy-policy">
	<p>
		This policy covers the consumer health data that Skin Groove LLC (“Skin
		Groove”, “we”, “us”) collects. It applies to each client, wherever you live.
		Our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> covers the rest of the information
		that we collect.
	</p>

	<h2>What we collect, and why</h2>
	<p>
		When you book, Cal.com records the consultation that you book, for example
		an acne consultation, with your name and contact details. We collect that
		because we need it to hold the consultation that you ask for. We collect the
		rest only after you consent in our intake form. The intake form and the
		check-in form before a follow-up collect:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Your skin concerns, skin history, and diagnosed skin conditions.</li>
		<li>Your medications, allergies, and pregnancy status.</li>
		<li>
			Your skincare routine, past treatments, and habits such as sun exposure
			and sleep.
		</li>
		<li>Photos of your skin.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		We use this data only to prepare for and hold your consultations, to give
		you skincare guidance and product recommendations, and to track your
		progress. We use a photo in our marketing only with your separate, optional
		permission. That permission can allow photos with identifiable features, or
		only photos with those features removed or hidden.
	</p>

	<h2>Where it comes from</h2>
	<p>
		You give it to us directly, in our forms, and in your emails, text messages,
		and consultations. For a client under 18, a parent or legal guardian also
		gives their consent in the intake form.
	</p>

	<h2>What we share, and with whom</h2>
	<p>
		We do not sell consumer health data. We share it with no affiliates, and
		with two third parties only:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Stripe, which processes the payment for your booking. It receives your
			name, your email address, and the consultation that you book. Stripe also
			uses payment data for its own purposes, such as fraud prevention and legal
			compliance, under its own privacy policy. We share this data because you
			need it to pay for the consultation that you ask for.
		</li>
		<li>
			The public, for a photo of your skin that you give us separate, optional
			permission to use in our marketing. We publish that photo only where your
			permission allows: our Instagram, our website, or our emails. There, the
			public can see it, and so can Meta, which runs Instagram.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>These service providers process this data only for us, under contract:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Tally, which stores our forms and your photos.</li>
		<li>
			Google Workspace, which carries our email and our calendar. Tally emails
			us a copy of each form submission.
		</li>
		<li>Cal.com, which runs our booking.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Text messages that you send us, and their photos, travel through mobile
		carriers, and through Apple if you use iMessage, to reach Sammy’s phone.
	</p>
	<p>
		We disclose consumer health data only when the law requires it, for example
		a valid court order. Before we share it for any other reason, we ask for
		your separate consent.
	</p>

	<h2>Your rights, and how to use them</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Confirm whether we collect your consumer health data.</li>
		<li>
			Get a copy of it, and a list of each service provider and third party that
			receives it, with an email address or an online contact for each.
		</li>
		<li>
			Withdraw your consent. We then stop collecting your data and delete the
			data that we hold, unless the law requires us to keep it.
		</li>
		<li>Ask us to delete it.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Email <a href={mailto}>{contactEmail}</a> to use these rights. A parent or
		guardian can use them for a client under 18. We answer within 45 days of
		receiving your request, and the time that we take to confirm your identity
		counts toward those 45 days. If we need more time, we tell you why within
		those 45 days, and we answer within 45 more days. If we refuse a request,
		you can appeal by a reply to our decision. If we deny your appeal, you can
		contact the attorney general of your state, or file a complaint with the{' '}
		<a href={washingtonComplaint}>Washington attorney general</a>.
	</p>
</article>
